---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import uofaStyleguideImage from '../../assets/images/uofa-styleguide.png';
import uofaBannerImage from '../../assets/images/uofa-banner.png';
import uofaSubpageImage from '../../assets/images/uofa-subpage.png';

const highlights = [
  { label: 'Organisation', value: 'University of Adelaide' },
  { label: 'Role', value: 'UX Designer & Front-End Developer' },
  { label: 'Scope', value: 'Custom styleguide application, Drupal CMS, all public-facing websites' },
];
---
<Layout title="Design Systems | Andrew Smith" description="Custom styleguide application built from scratch at the University of Adelaide — component library, documentation, and governance across the entire university web ecosystem." ogType="article" ogImageAlt="University of Adelaide styleguide application">
  <a class="skip-link" href="#main-content">Skip to content</a>
  <div class="case-page">
    <SiteHeader />
    <main id="main-content">
      <section class="case-hero">
        <p class="eyebrow"><a class="breadcrumb-link" href="/work">Work</a><span class="breadcrumb-separator" aria-hidden="true">/</span><span>Design Systems</span></p>
        <h1 class="display-title">Give a design a memory and a hundred screens will share it.</h1>
        <div class="hero-subgrid">
          <p class="hero-text">A design system is institutional memory made operational. Without it, every designer and developer makes the same decisions from scratch, introduces inconsistencies that cost development time, and builds interfaces where the quality depends entirely on who built it last.</p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="meta-strip" id="overview">
        {highlights.map((item) => (<div class="meta-item"><p class="meta-label">{item.label}</p><p class="meta-value">{item.value}</p></div>))}
      </section>
      <section class="lead-image-section">
        <Image class="lead-image" src={uofaStyleguideImage} alt="University of Adelaide custom styleguide application showing Audio component documentation with HTML, CSS, and JavaScript code examples" decoding="async" widths={[720, 1080, 1440]} sizes="(max-width: 78rem) 100vw, 78rem" />
      </section>
      <section class="summary-section">
        <p class="section-kicker">The Challenge</p>
        <h2>Every development team building separately, from scratch, without a shared foundation.</h2>
        <div class="summary-main">
          <p>The University of Adelaide had multiple development teams building public-facing websites and internal systems independently. Each team had their own approach to markup, styling, and JavaScript. The result was inconsistency across the university's digital presence, inaccessible interfaces, and enormous amounts of duplicated effort.</p>
          <p>The solution was to build a custom styleguide application that every team could reference — a single, maintained source of truth for how interfaces should be built across the university.</p>
          <p class="summary-meta"><strong>Technical scope:</strong> HTML, CSS, JavaScript, Drupal CMS integration, responsive design, accessibility standards</p>
          <p class="summary-meta"><strong>Scale:</strong> Used across all public-facing websites and internal systems across the university</p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="editorial-section" id="process">
        <div class="section-rail"><p class="section-kicker">What Was Built</p></div>
        <div class="story-block">
          <p>The styleguide application documented every component across the university's digital ecosystem — layouts, navigation, containers, typography, forms, media, and interactive elements. Each component included the HTML markup, CSS classes, JavaScript behaviours, accessibility requirements, and a live demonstration in context.</p>
          <p>Development teams could either reference a minified version of the assets directly, or pull specific components and templates from the Git repository and customise them for their context. Either way, they were building on a tested, documented foundation rather than inventing their own.</p>
          <p>Building this required both the UX thinking (what are the right patterns, how should components behave, what are the accessibility requirements) and the front-end development skills to implement them correctly and document them clearly. That combination — designer and developer in one role — is what made the system credible to both teams.</p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="supporting-image-section">
        <Image class="supporting-image" src={uofaBannerImage} alt="University of Adelaide website shown across desktop, tablet, and mobile devices" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[720, 1080, 1440]} sizes="(max-width: 78rem) 100vw, 78rem" />
      </section>
      <section class="editorial-section">
        <div class="section-rail"><p class="section-kicker">The Output</p></div>
        <div class="story-block">
          <p>The result was a consistent visual language across the university's public presence — the same navigation patterns, typography system, and component behaviour on every page, regardless of which team built it. Accessibility was baked into the components rather than audited retrospectively.</p>
          <p>The subpage below shows the design system in action — the same header, navigation, typography, and layout patterns that every team across the university used as their starting point.</p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="supporting-image-section">
        <Image class="supporting-image" src={uofaSubpageImage} alt="University of Adelaide Research Institutes subpage showing the styleguide design system in production" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[720, 1080, 1440]} sizes="(max-width: 78rem) 100vw, 78rem" />
      </section>
    </main>
  </div>
</Layout>
<style>
  .case-page { position: relative; width: min(calc(100% - 3rem), 78rem); margin: 0 auto; padding: 1.25rem 0 5rem; }
  .eyebrow { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.82rem; font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--muted-foreground); margin: 0 0 1.25rem; display: flex; gap: 0.5rem; align-items: center; }
  .breadcrumb-link { font-weight: 600; } .breadcrumb-separator { color: var(--muted-foreground); }
  h1, h2 { margin: 0; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: -0.05em; color: var(--foreground); }
  .display-title { font-size: clamp(2rem, 4.5vw, 3.5rem); line-height: 1.06; margin: 1rem 0 0; max-width: 28ch; }
  .hero-subgrid { margin-top: 1.75rem; }
  .hero-text, .story-block p, .summary-main p, .summary-meta { font-size: 1.08rem; line-height: 1.7; margin: 0 0 1rem; }
  .case-hero { padding: 5rem 0 2rem; }
  .meta-strip { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 2rem; padding: 1.5rem 0 0.5rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--border); }
  .meta-label { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.78rem; font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: 0.06em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--muted-foreground); margin: 0; }
  .meta-value { margin: 0.35rem 0 0; font-size: 1.1rem; font-weight: 700; }
  .lead-image-section { padding: 2rem 0 4rem; }
  .lead-image, .supporting-image { width: 100%; border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 1.5rem; box-shadow: var(--shadow-large); }
  .supporting-image-section { padding: 2rem 0 4rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); }
  .summary-section { padding: 0 0 3rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); }
  .summary-section h2 { margin-top: 0.75rem; font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 2.5vw, 2.2rem); max-width: 32ch; }
  .summary-main { margin-top: 1.5rem; max-width: 44rem; }
  .section-kicker { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.78rem; font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: 0.06em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--muted-foreground); margin: 0 0 0.5rem; }
  .editorial-section { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 14rem minmax(0,1fr); gap: 2rem; padding: 3rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); }
  .story-block { max-width: 44rem; }
</style>
